## Building an Acronym Generator

### The Basic Algorithm

Take the first letter of each word. "National Aeronautics and Space Administration" → "NAASA". But wait — articles and prepositions ('and', 'of', 'the') are usually skipped: → "NASA".

### Implementation

```xiom
fn generate_acronym(phrase: Str) -> Str {
  let skip_words: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  skip_words.push("and"); skip_words.push("of");
  skip_words.push("the"); skip_words.push("in");
  skip_words.push("for"); skip_words.push("a");

  let mut acronym = "";
  let mut current_word = "";
  let mut i = 0;

  while i < phrase.len() {
    let c = phrase.get(i).unwrap();
    if c == ' ' {
      if current_word.len() > 0 && !should_skip(current_word, &skip_words) {
        let first = current_word.get(0).unwrap();
        let upper = to_upper(first);
        acronym = string.str_concat(acronym, string.from_char(upper));
      };
      current_word = "";
    } else {
      current_word = string.str_concat(current_word, string.from_char(c));
    };
    i += 1;
  };

  if current_word.len() > 0 && !should_skip(current_word, &skip_words) {
    let first = current_word.get(0).unwrap();
    let upper = to_upper(first);
    acronym = string.str_concat(acronym, string.from_char(upper));
  };

  acronym
}

fn should_skip(word: Str, skip_words: &Vec[Str]) -> Bool {
  let mut i = 0;
  while i < skip_words.len() {
    if word == skip_words.get(i).unwrap() { return true; };
    i += 1;
  };
  false
}
```

### Handling Hyphenated Words

"State-of-the-Art" → take first letter of EACH part: "SOTA". Split on hyphens too, treating each segment as a separate word.

